{"name":"Elizabeth Rose","hex":"#fadfd2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elizabeth Rose (nun)","blocks":[{"text":"Elizabeth Rose was a Benedictine nun at Chelles, France. She founded the convent of Sainte-Marie-du-Rozoy, near Courtenay, Loiret, France, and served as its first abbess. Eventually she retired to live as an anchoress in a hollow oak tree."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Rose_(nun)"},{"title":"Elizabeth Rose, Lady of Kilravock","blocks":[{"text":"Elizabeth Rose, 19th Baroness of Kilravock was an eighteenth-century Scottish literary critic and author."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Rose%2C_Lady_of_Kilravock"},{"title":"Elizabeth Rose (academic)","blocks":[{"text":"Elizabeth Louise Rose is an American academic, and is Professor in Business Policy and Strategy at the Indian Institute of Management Udaipur. Rose's research focuses on international business, covering topics such as how small and medium size enterprises internationalise, and how firms compete across borders."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Rose_(academic)"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"